(292 observations deleted)

.         keep if (kidgeb02>0 & kidmon02>0  & kidsex01>0) | kidgeb02==-2 // Inf
> o on second child
(0 observations deleted)

. 
. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         mvdecode plh0298_v2 kidgeb01  kidmon01  kidsex01 kidsex02, mv(-1/-9) 
> // negatives to missing
    kidsex02: 12 missing values generated

.         mvdecode kidgeb02 kidmon02, mv(-1, -3/-7) // negatives to missing, ex
> cept -2 (no second child)

.         recode kidgeb02 (-2=0) // zero if no second child
(12 changes made to kidgeb02)

.         replace kidgeb02 = 0 if kidgeb02>2018 | (kidgeb02==2018 & kidmon02>pg
> month & syear==2018) // zero if second child born after interview
(5 real changes made)

.         replace kidsex02=0 if kidgeb02==0 // don't use second childs gender i
> f born after interview
(17 real changes made)

.         
.         recode plh0298_v2 (1=7 "Stimme überhaupt nicht zu") (2=6) (3=5) (4=4)
>  (5=3) (6=2) (7=1 "Stimme voll zu"), gen(Y)
(15 differences between plh0298_v2 and Y)

.         label var Y "Children under age of 6 suffer if mother works"

.         
.         recode kidsex01 (1=0 "Son") (2=1 "Daughter"), gen(d_daughter) // 1st 
> Child gender variable
(19 differences between kidsex01 and d_daughter)

.         label var d_daughter "1st Child Daughter"

.         
.         recode kidsex02 (.=0 "No second child") (1=1 "Son") (2=2 "Daughter"),
>  gen(second_gender) // 2nd Child gender variable
(0 differences between kidsex02 and second_gender)

.         label var second_gender "Gender of second child"

. 
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * Our outcomevariable is Y = Children under age of 6 suffer if mother
>  works
.         * We want to test whether having a daughter affects this outcome, and
>  we want to know whether the birth year (or time passed since birth, which ar
> e identical here) changes this effect. We also want to control for potential 
> additional children born before the outcome information was collected. We wan
> t to test this effect separately for men and women.
.         * We hence run two linear OLS models on Y (one for men, one for women
> ) including a dummy variable for the first child being a daughter, which we i
> nteract with birth year. We also include a control for the gender of the seco
> nd child.
.         
.         reg Y i.d_daughter##i.kidgeb01 i.second_gender if sex ==1 // Men
note: 2017.kidgeb01 omitted because of collinearity.
note: 0b.d_daughter#2017.kidgeb01 identifies no observations in the sample.
note: 1.d_daughter#2016b.kidgeb01 identifies no observations in the sample.
note: 1.d_daughter#2017.kidgeb01 omitted because of collinearity.

